results/' + $('#other_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/312"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "Team All-Around, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "12 – 13 August 1948 — 9:00"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/341"} "Empress Hall, Earls Court Exhibition Centre, Kensington, London"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/70136"} "123 from 16 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "Eight-person teams. Best six individual total scores to count towards team score."]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Jury"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/21259"} "Félix, Comte Goblet d'Alviella"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/BEL"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/BEL.png"}] "BEL"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Jury"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/31169"} "Miroslav Klinger"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/TCH"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/TCH.png"}] "TCH"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Jury"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/2306263"} "M. C. Lapalu"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/FRA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/FRA.png"}] "FRA"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Jury"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/30670"} "Pierre Hentges"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/LUX"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/LUX.png"}] "LUX"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Sixteen (16) nations were entered with eight-man teams, although Argentina and Cuba had only seven team members, and Mexico only five. The team score was made up of the six best all-around scores among the eight team members. With no international competition since the 1938 World Championships, it was difficult to choose a favorite. Germany had won the gold medal at " [:a {:href "/results/70114"} "Berlin in 1936"] ", but was not present at London in 1948, barred because of its war record."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The competition was remarkably close, with Finland winning the gold medal over Switzerland by only 1.60 points – 1,358.30 to 1,356.70. Hungary was a distant third to win the bronze medal, while France was well back in fourth. Finland was led by the " [:a {:href "/results/70135"} "individual"] " gold medalist " [:a {:href "/athletes/29778"} "Veikko Huhtanen"] ", with another team member, " [:a {:href "/athletes/29833"} "Heikki Savolainen"] ", competing in his fourth Olympics, and he would return again in " [:a {:href "/editions/13/sports/GAR"} "1952"] ", eventually winning nine Olympic medals. Another Finn, " [:a {:href "/athletes/29851"} "Einari Teräsvirta"] ", had also competed in " [:a {:href "/editions/10/sports/GAR"} "1932"] " and " [:a {:href "/editions/11/sports/GAR"} "1936"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The Swiss had four of the top six individual performers, but the consistency of the Finnish team brought them the gold medal. They were led by " [:a {:href "/athletes/31027"} "Walter Lehmann"] ", silver medalist in the individual all-around. The final scoring member for Switzerland was " [:a {:href "/athletes/31040"} "Michael Reusch"] ", who won two silver medals at Berlin in 1936, and three apparatus gold medals at the 1938 World Championships."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitors"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Points"] [:th {} "Compulsory Points"] [:th {} "Optional Points"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :st